00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.640
So, the one thing that all mammals have&nbsp;
in common is that they all have mammary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.520
glands (which allows females to produce&nbsp;
milk for their offspring). However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:38.400
not all mammals give birth to live babies. A few&nbsp;
species—such as the playtypus and the echidnas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:45.440
(both in the Monotremata Order)—actually lay eggs,&nbsp;
just like birds and reptiles do. However, the vast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:51.280
majority of mammals are in fact livebearers&nbsp;
and they belong to a subclass called Theria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:57.520
which simply means “beasts”. From here, things&nbsp;
go in two very different directions. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.720
are the marsupials, who have a very short&nbsp;
gestation period, after which their babies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:08.800
continue to develop in an external pouch; and&nbsp;
the placentals, who have a much longer gestation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:14.000
period (because they have placentas and are&nbsp;
thus able feed their babies in the womb).

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.800
Let’s start with the marsupials (which are&nbsp;
shown in purple) as they are the smaller of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:24.000
the two groups. Now, take note, I’m not going&nbsp;
to be pointing out everything as this is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
a draft version of the chart and I’ll be doing a&nbsp;
more in-depth video sometime next year. But I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.440
going to hit all of the highlights so that you&nbsp;
can tell me if I missed anything important. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:40.560
although most marsupials live in Australia,&nbsp;
there are two types that live in the Americas:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:46.400
opposums and shrew opposums (each of which&nbsp;
are placed in their own orders). By the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:52.640
that number shown below each order name represents&nbsp;
the total number of species in that order. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.480
all of the remaining marsupials belong&nbsp;
to the Australidelphia superorder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:05.600
which is made up of 5 orders; although most of&nbsp;
the familiar species are found in Diprotodontia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:12.080
such as koalas, wallabies, kangaroos,&nbsp;
the ever so cute quokkas, and wombats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:17.680
The only well known Australian marsupial that&nbsp;
doesn’t belong to this order is the Tasmanian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:24.480
devil, which is in Dasyuromorphia, along&nbsp;
with the recently extinct Tasmanian tiger.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:30.320
Moving over to the placentals, you'll notice that&nbsp;
things immediately split again in two directions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:35.840
This is because, during the early days of mammal&nbsp;
evolution, there were only two main continents on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.400
earth: a northern one and a southern one.&nbsp;
Placentals that trace their origin to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:46.160
northern continent are placed in Boreoeutheria&nbsp;
(boreo meaning northern) whereas those that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:52.800
trace their origin to the southern continent are&nbsp;
placed in Atlantogenata (atlanto being a reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:59.680
to the Atlantic ocean—which first formed when the&nbsp;
southern continent broke in two). The western half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:06.080
of that continent became South America; whereas&nbsp;
the eastern half became Africa—which is why we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:13.200
get yet another split here. The South American&nbsp;
branch is called Xenarthra, which means “strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:19.680
joints” and it is so-named because the animals in&nbsp;
this clade have extra joints in their backbones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:28.080
It contains two small orders: the armadillo order&nbsp;
and Pilosa, which includes sloths and anteaters.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:34.800
Afrotheria is slightly bigger, containing&nbsp;
6 small orders, the most famous of which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:41.600
Proboscidea (named after the proboscis—meaning&nbsp;
a long nose or tube). These are, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.760
the elephants, of which there&nbsp;
are only 3 species remaining:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:53.120
two in Africa and one in Asia. However, take&nbsp;
note that in Afrotheria, we also find hyraxes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.120 --> 00:05:00.480
sea cows (such as manatees and dugongs) and&nbsp;
several insectivores (such as the aardvark).

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:05.280
Now, before I continue, I want to mention that&nbsp;
most of the individual images on this chart were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:11.840
taken from the website iNaturalist (but only from&nbsp;
observations with photos marked public domain).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:17.200
This video is not sponsored by iNaturalist but&nbsp;
since it’s playing an important role in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.480
project, I do want to shout it out. Basically,&nbsp;
iNaturalist is for anyone interested in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:27.760
natural world, whether they be professionals&nbsp;
or amateurs. This is because it has an app that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:34.480
anyone can use to identify and make a record of&nbsp;
things observed in nature (such as wild animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:40.720
birds, insects, mushrooms, plants, et cetera).&nbsp;
I’m an active user of the app and if you are too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:46.560
you can find me on there as matt hyphen b. I’d&nbsp;
especially appreciate it if you have any photos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:51.520
you’d like to contribute to the project. Now,&nbsp;
if you haven’t ever used iNaturalist but are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:55.920
interested in learning more, there’s actually&nbsp;
something coming up called iNat Together where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:01.680
you find an event near you and show up in person&nbsp;
to explore nature with others and learn how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:07.520
use the app. I’ll drop a link in the description&nbsp;
which you can use to search events by location.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:13.200
There’s even one happening near me, which I’ll&nbsp;
be participating in. So maybe I’ll see you there.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:19.680
Okay, back to the chart. Let’s now move over to&nbsp;
Boreoeutheria, which is where we find over 85%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:25.920
of all mammal species. As you can see, things&nbsp;
once again split in two directions. On the left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:32.000
(shown in green) is Laurasiatheria (Laurasia&nbsp;
being the name of that northern continent I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.920
mentioned earlier) and on the right (shown&nbsp;
in orange and red) is Eu-archonto-glires,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:44.560
which gets its name because it gets split even&nbsp;
further into Eu-archonta (which means true rulers)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:51.440
and Glires (which means dormice). Let’s look at&nbsp;
Glires first. It contains the Lagomorphia Order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:57.600
which is where we find rabbits and hares; and&nbsp;
the Rodentia Order, which is where we find,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:05.680
well, rodents. At over 2300 species, the rodent&nbsp;
order is the largest mammal order by far. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:11.440
it doesn’t just contain rats and mice. In&nbsp;
this suborder, we get beavers and gophers;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:17.040
in this one, we get squirrels and their relatives&nbsp;
(such as groundhogs, prairie dogs, and chipmunks);&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:21.840
in this one, we get porcupines, guinea&nbsp;
pigs, and capybaras; and in this one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:27.680
we get the ones more closely related to&nbsp;
rats and mice, such as gerbils and hamsters.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:34.240
Now, when it comes to euarchonta (the “true&nbsp;
rulers”), most belong to the Primate Order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:40.160
(which means top ranking) however there's also&nbsp;
two smaller orders, the tree shrews and the flying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:47.760
lemurs. But yeah, it's the Primate Order (shown in&nbsp;
red) which is the main one, with over 500 species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:54.240
(including humans) so let's take a close look&nbsp;
at them. Primates are first of all divided into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:01.760
Strepsirrhini (which literally means “wet nosed”)&nbsp;
and Haplorhini (which means simple or dry nosed).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:09.840
In Strepsirrhini, we get lemurs, lorises, and bush&nbsp;
babies; whereas in Haplorhini we get tarsiers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:15.840
simians (simian being a category that includes&nbsp;
both monkeys and apes). But at this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:22.720
things do not divide into monkeys and apes, they&nbsp;
divide into Platyrrhini (which means “flat-nosed”)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:28.160
and Catarrhini (which means “down-nosed”).&nbsp;
Perhaps more importantly though, Platyrrhini&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:35.440
contains all of the New World simians, whereas&nbsp;
Catarrhini contains all of the Old World simians.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:42.160
In Platyrrhini, there are no apes but there are&nbsp;
five distinct monkey families, the two main ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:50.240
being Cebidae (which includes the capuchins and&nbsp;
squirrel monkeys) and (which includes the spider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:58.240
monkeys and howlers). In contrast, in Catarrhini,&nbsp;
there is only one monkey family but also two ape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:05.520
families. The Old World monkey family is called&nbsp;
Cercopithecidae and it includes baboons and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:12.160
rhesus monkeys, among many others. As for the&nbsp;
apes, there's the gibbon family and then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:20.000
“greater ape” family, which is called Hominidae.&nbsp;
Currently, Hominidae consists of eight species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:26.640
divided amongst four genera. There's Homo&nbsp;
(which is us), Pan (which is the chimpanzees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:33.360
and bonobos), Gorilla (which is the two gorilla&nbsp;
species—eastern and western), and Pongo (which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:40.400
is the three orangutan species, one of which,&nbsp;
P. tapanuliensis, was only discovered in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:46.720
late 90s). Now, because this particular family is&nbsp;
so important to us (because, well, we're in it),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:51.360
there's obviously been a lot of research done&nbsp;
on its evolutionary history. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:57.840
when it comes to extinct species, there's around&nbsp;
20 more species that could be added. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:03.040
since this particular chart is mostly focused&nbsp;
only on living species, I opted not to go down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:08.880
that road. Sure, I have included a few extinct&nbsp;
species on this chart but they are all ones that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:14.320
have gone extinct during the historical&nbsp;
era, as opposed to pre-historical times.

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:19.440
Alright, so that takes care of everything&nbsp;
in Euarchontaglires, which means that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:25.840
now time to move over to the Laurasiatheria&nbsp;
side, which starts in green. Right of the bat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:31.840
the Eulipotyphla Order splits off with&nbsp;
everything else being called Scrotifera.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:36.960
Members of Eulipotyphla (which include&nbsp;
shrews, hedgehogs, and moles) are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:43.280
known as true insectivores because they form&nbsp;
the largest group of insect-eaters. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:47.680
this order used to be called Insectivora&nbsp;
and it used to include a bunch of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:53.520
shrew-like species, which have now (based&nbsp;
on DNA comparison) been placed elsewhere.

00:10:53.520 --> 00:11:00.320
Okay, the next order to split off is Chiroptera,&nbsp;
which is where we find bats—the only mammals with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:06.320
the ability to fly. The bat order is actually&nbsp;
the second largest mammal order, surpassed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:12.320
only by the rodents. It is used to be divided&nbsp;
into macrobats and microbats but now the terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:21.280
Yinptero-chiroptera and Yango-chiroptera are used.&nbsp;
Yinptero-chiroptera includes all of the macrobats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:29.200
(such as flying foxes) but also a few microbat&nbsp;
families, such as the horseshoe bats and roundleaf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:35.920
bats. The vast majority of microbats however are&nbsp;
now placed in Yango-chiroptera, with the largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:44.080
family being Vespertilionidae (which just means&nbsp;
bats, or more literally, night flyers). The two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:51.280
most common species found in North America are the&nbsp;
big brown bat and the little brown bat. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:59.040
it is in this family here that we find the famous&nbsp;
vampire bats, who feed exclusively on blood.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:06.000
Okay, moving up, we come to Ferungulata, so&nbsp;
named because it contains the so-called “wild”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:12.320
beasts (Ferae) as well as the ungulates&nbsp;
(or hoofed animals). Let’s look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:20.560
ungulates first. They are split into two orders:&nbsp;
Perissodactyla (which literally means odd-toed)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:27.360
and Artiodactyla (which means even-toed). In&nbsp;
the odd-toed group, we get rhinos, tapirs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:33.760
and eequines (which include horses, donkeys, and&nbsp;
zebras). But it’s in the even-toed group that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:40.160
get most of the hoofed animals (as well as a bit&nbsp;
of a surprise). First off, there’s the pig family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:46.720
as well as the camel family (which not only&nbsp;
includes camels but also llamas and alpacas).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:53.120
Then we get a group called Cetruminantia (so named&nbsp;
because it combines the prefix Cet- (which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:59.760
whale) with the word ruminant. You see, if we go&nbsp;
this way, we come to Whippomorpha, which includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:06.880
hippos but also Cetaceans (or whales). So, yeah,&nbsp;
basically, the ancestor of all whales was some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:14.000
sort of even-toed hoofed animal. Which is why they&nbsp;
are now placed within Artiodactyla instead of in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:20.400
their own order. However, because they are so&nbsp;
different, I opted to give them a special color.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:27.600
When it comes to whales, we have two main types:&nbsp;
toothed whales and baleen whales. Basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:32.560
instead of teeth, baleen whales&nbsp;
have a row of fibres called baleens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:37.040
which serve a similar purpose.&nbsp;
This group includes grey whales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:42.080
humpback whales, and the largest&nbsp;
animals on earth: the blue whales.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:49.680
In contrast, toothed whales include sperm whales,&nbsp;
belugas &amp; narwals, and all of the dolphins.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:55.040
Let’s now look at the ruminants, who all&nbsp;
share the trait of chewing their cud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.040 --> 00:14:01.440
which is a multiple step process for digesting&nbsp;
food. Ruminants include giraffes but the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:09.280
main families are the cervids and the bovids. In&nbsp;
Cervidae, we get deer but also moose, caribou,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:17.920
and elk; and in Bovidae we get bovines (such as&nbsp;
cows, bison, and buffalos) but also wildebeests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:24.000
impalas, gazelles, and caprines&nbsp;
(which include both sheep and goats).

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:29.840
Alright, moving over to the wild beasts.&nbsp;
Basically, everything here belongs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:35.680
the Carnivora Order (which I’ve shown in&nbsp;
yellow), except for this small order here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:43.520
which includes the pangolins. Carnivora is divided&nbsp;
into the Feliformia (or cat-like) suborder and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:51.520
the Caniformia (or dog-like) suborder. On the&nbsp;
cat-like side, we get civets, hyenas, mongooses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:59.120
and of course, cats. The cat family is called&nbsp;
Felidae and I’ve chosen to show five of 14 felid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:05.200
genera: Lynx (which is where we find bobcats),&nbsp;
Felis (which is where we find the domestic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:11.280
cat—represented by one of my own cats, named&nbsp;
Ketsl), Puma (which is where we find cougars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:17.920
or mountain lions), Acinonyx (which is where&nbsp;
we find cheetahs), and Panthera (which is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:25.840
the find the largest cats). Panthera has a total&nbsp;
of five species and I’ve chosen to show them all:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:31.440
the jaguar, the tiger, the lion,&nbsp;
the leopard, and the snow leopard.

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:36.320
Finally, let’s look at the dog-like&nbsp;
carnivores. Of course, there’s Canidae,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:42.400
the main dog family (which includes foxes,&nbsp;
jackals, coyotes, and wolves, from whence came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:48.960
the domestic dog). But there’s also the pinnipeds&nbsp;
(which include walruses, sea lions, and seals),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:55.840
the bear family, and the musteloids—which&nbsp;
is where we get skunks, racoons, red pandas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.840 --> 00:16:04.480
and the entire Mustelidae Family (which includes&nbsp;
wolverines, badgers, minks, weasels, and otters).

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:11.840
Okay, so that was a look at our brand new&nbsp;
Classification of Mammals chart. Like I say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:18.080
though, this is just a first draft and I’ll&nbsp;
be making changes based on your comments. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:23.040
if you have any suggestions, do let me&nbsp;
know. And if you want to pre-order the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:28.480
chart, be sure to click the link in the&nbsp;
description or pinned comment right now.

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:44.000
Thanks for watching.

